1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
Complex refineries separated from oil,

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:08,000
a massive infrastructure delivers it to every service station in America.

3
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:12,000
Without gasoline, modern life would grind to a halt.

4
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:16,000
More than 100 years of science and sweat

5
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:22,000
have transformed it from an unwanted waste product to our most vital fossil fuel.

6
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:26,000
Now, Gasoline on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,000
Where would we be without gasoline?

8
00:00:37,001 --> 00:00:39,000
Put away the portion.

9
00:00:39,001 --> 00:00:41,000
No more NASCAR?

10
00:00:41,001 --> 00:00:43,000
So long speedboat.

11
00:00:43,001 --> 00:00:46,000
Hang up the Harley.

12
00:00:46,001 --> 00:00:48,000
Ground that plane.

13
00:00:48,001 --> 00:00:51,000
Take away the tank.

14
00:00:51,001 --> 00:00:55,000
Gasoline fuels the world, and not just the fast and the furious.

15
00:00:55,001 --> 00:00:58,040
It's just the fast and the furious.

16
00:00:58,041 --> 00:01:02,200
Businesses, commuters, and even soccer moms depend on it.

17
00:01:02,201 --> 00:01:05,400
Worldwide, gasoline is extremely important.

18
00:01:05,401 --> 00:01:08,940
Third world countries are aspiring to become modern,

19
00:01:08,941 --> 00:01:12,960
and as they do so, the demand for gasoline goes up.

20
00:01:12,961 --> 00:01:16,360
Mobility is very important.

21
00:01:16,361 --> 00:01:18,160
And for better or worse,

22
00:01:18,161 --> 00:01:20,560
Americans rely more heavily on gasoline

23
00:01:20,561 --> 00:01:22,500
than any other country.

24
00:01:22,501 --> 00:01:24,100
In Europe and some other countries,

25
00:01:24,101 --> 00:01:26,440
emphasis on public transportation.

26
00:01:26,540 --> 00:01:28,680
Here in the United States, people

27
00:01:28,780 --> 00:01:31,200
are used to taking their cars everywhere they go.

28
00:01:31,300 --> 00:01:35,040
So if suddenly gasoline disappeared from the universe,

29
00:01:35,140 --> 00:01:37,440
Americans would have a pretty hard time going

30
00:01:37,540 --> 00:01:39,940
about their daily lives.

31
00:01:40,040 --> 00:01:43,780
Drivers in the USA use about 360 million gallons

32
00:01:43,880 --> 00:01:47,360
of gasoline every single day.

33
00:01:47,460 --> 00:01:50,000
That's big business, to say the least.

34
00:01:50,100 --> 00:01:53,760
Without gasoline, modern cities would not exist.

35
00:01:53,761 --> 00:01:55,260
in the way they do in the US.

36
00:01:55,261 --> 00:01:58,060
We built a mass transit system around one or two people

37
00:01:58,061 --> 00:02:02,260
driving on automobiles all around a large metropolitan area.

38
00:02:02,261 --> 00:02:04,840
We're making adjustments to that, needed adjustments,

39
00:02:04,841 --> 00:02:08,680
carpooling, but it's hard to imagine a time when our economy

40
00:02:08,681 --> 00:02:14,440
won't rely heavily on gasoline and gasoline-powered vehicles.

41
00:02:14,441 --> 00:02:17,280
One reason why gasoline will be around for a long time

42
00:02:17,281 --> 00:02:21,180
is that it provides a lot of power.

43
00:02:21,181 --> 00:02:26,280
One gallon of gasoline provides the equivalent of more than 36,000 watt hours of energy,

44
00:02:26,680 --> 00:02:29,000
enough to run a colored television for more than a month.

45
00:02:30,080 --> 00:02:34,860
There are some alternatives that provide even more energy, like hydrogen-powered fuel cells.

46
00:02:35,540 --> 00:02:41,660
But right now, gasoline gives us all the other things we need in a fuel that the other options can't yet match.

47
00:02:42,600 --> 00:02:46,520
We're asking it to provide low emissions, good fuel economy.

48
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:50,420
We're asking it to not contribute to deposit buildup in the engine.

49
00:02:50,421 --> 00:02:53,420
contribute to the failure of emissions control equipment.

50
00:02:53,520 --> 00:02:55,820
We want it to be fully available when we want it

51
00:02:55,920 --> 00:02:58,320
and as low a cost as possible.

52
00:02:58,420 --> 00:03:00,460
That lowest possible price isn't always

53
00:03:00,560 --> 00:03:02,660
as cheap as we'd like.

54
00:03:02,760 --> 00:03:05,460
But considering all the steps gasoline goes through before it

55
00:03:05,560 --> 00:03:10,380
enters your tank, it's still a pretty good bargain.

56
00:03:10,480 --> 00:03:14,080
Crude oil has to be found, brought up from thousands of

57
00:03:14,180 --> 00:03:18,180
feet below the surface, shipped thousands of miles

58
00:03:18,181 --> 00:03:21,180
miles to a billion-dollar refinery, squeezed through all

59
00:03:21,280 --> 00:03:24,720
of that expensive refining equipment, moved to cities

60
00:03:24,820 --> 00:03:28,160
through thousands of miles of underground pipelines,

61
00:03:28,260 --> 00:03:32,120
pumped into tanker trucks, and delivered to service stations.

62
00:03:32,220 --> 00:03:34,560
Look at the price of a gallon of gasoline,

63
00:03:34,660 --> 00:03:36,800
especially after you subtract the taxes,

64
00:03:36,900 --> 00:03:39,460
versus the price of a gallon of bottled water.

65
00:03:39,560 --> 00:03:43,300
Gasoline is around a dollar a gallon, plus or minus,

66
00:03:43,400 --> 00:03:45,040
after you subtract taxes.

67
00:03:45,041 --> 00:03:48,480
And we're hard-pressed to find even a liter of bottled water

68
00:03:48,580 --> 00:03:52,080
that isn't well over a dollar a gallon.

69
00:03:52,180 --> 00:03:54,380
Obviously, bottled water makers aren't selling

70
00:03:54,480 --> 00:03:57,180
360 million gallons a day.

71
00:03:57,280 --> 00:03:59,680
But the main reason gasoline is cheaper

72
00:03:59,780 --> 00:04:03,300
is an efficient production and delivery infrastructure.

73
00:04:03,400 --> 00:04:06,300
And the modern refinery is at the heart of that system.

74
00:04:08,500 --> 00:04:11,800
Refineries are huge, billion-dollar complexes,

75
00:04:11,801 --> 00:04:15,740
often covering the area of 100 football fields.

76
00:04:15,840 --> 00:04:17,800
In a single day, a large refinery

77
00:04:17,900 --> 00:04:22,040
can transform 400,000 barrels of crude oil

78
00:04:22,140 --> 00:04:24,440
into millions of gallons of gasoline

79
00:04:24,540 --> 00:04:26,540
and other refined products.

80
00:04:26,640 --> 00:04:28,860
Despite their size, all of the work

81
00:04:28,960 --> 00:04:31,860
is being done on a molecular scale.

82
00:04:31,960 --> 00:04:33,560
The crude oil fed into the refinery

83
00:04:33,660 --> 00:04:37,300
is made almost entirely of hydrocarbons,

84
00:04:37,400 --> 00:04:40,560
chains of carbon atoms in various lengths,

85
00:04:40,561 --> 00:04:43,660
surrounded by hydrogen.

86
00:04:43,760 --> 00:04:46,260
Inside the refinery, all of these hydrocarbon chains

87
00:04:46,360 --> 00:04:49,060
are separated in what are known as fractionating towers

88
00:04:49,160 --> 00:04:53,800
or columns, one of the most distinctive features

89
00:04:53,900 --> 00:04:54,700
of any refinery.

90
00:04:57,520 --> 00:05:00,620
Furnaces next to the tower heat the oil to about 700 degrees

91
00:05:00,720 --> 00:05:02,620
Fahrenheit.

92
00:05:02,720 --> 00:05:06,820
The hydrocarbons boil off like steam from shortest to longest,

93
00:05:06,821 --> 00:05:11,060
except for the thickest semi-solid products.

94
00:05:11,160 --> 00:05:14,220
And there are a series of trays in the column.

95
00:05:14,320 --> 00:05:17,360
So the lighter product will rise as a gas

96
00:05:17,460 --> 00:05:19,460
through what's called bubblers.

97
00:05:19,560 --> 00:05:23,980
They actually allow a liquid level to stay on the tray.

98
00:05:24,080 --> 00:05:27,080
The gas comes through until it comes to the point where it

99
00:05:27,180 --> 00:05:30,480
condenses at that level of cooling.

100
00:05:30,580 --> 00:05:33,880
We can draw off those fractions.

101
00:05:33,980 --> 00:05:36,080
The lighter materials have just a few carbons

102
00:05:36,081 --> 00:05:40,680
in each molecule, for example, propane with three carbons

103
00:05:40,681 --> 00:05:44,020
and butane, a four-carbon chain.

104
00:05:44,021 --> 00:05:46,020
These will go right out of the top of the tower

105
00:05:46,021 --> 00:05:48,620
since they are still in a gaseous form,

106
00:05:48,621 --> 00:05:52,640
even after they cool back down to normal temperatures.

107
00:05:52,641 --> 00:05:55,940
Gasoline is mostly comprised of fairly light molecules

108
00:05:55,941 --> 00:05:59,840
with between six and 10 carbons.

109
00:05:59,841 --> 00:06:01,840
They rise nearly to the top of the column

110
00:06:01,841 --> 00:06:04,240
before condensing back to liquid.

111
00:06:04,241 --> 00:06:07,500
The liquid is then removed through side tubes.

112
00:06:07,501 --> 00:06:13,700
Kerosene, slightly heavier, weighs in at 12 to 15 carbons.

113
00:06:13,701 --> 00:06:16,800
Diesel fuel settles and condenses just below that,

114
00:06:16,801 --> 00:06:20,600
since it's in the 15 to 18 carbon range.

115
00:06:20,601 --> 00:06:23,300
Lubricating oils, including engine motor oils,

116
00:06:23,301 --> 00:06:25,800
are even thicker, since they have longer chains

117
00:06:25,801 --> 00:06:28,680
than gasoline or diesel.

118
00:06:28,681 --> 00:06:31,240
And the heavyweight products, with 20 or more carbons

119
00:06:31,241 --> 00:06:36,240
carbons in each chain collect at the bottom of the tower.

120
00:06:36,340 --> 00:06:39,640
These materials, such as paraffin wax and tar,

121
00:06:39,740 --> 00:06:43,380
are in a semi-solid state and never totally vaporize.

122
00:06:43,480 --> 00:06:47,020
That's a lot of diversity for something referred to as crude.

123
00:06:47,120 --> 00:06:50,220
It is a progression out of a barrel of crude oil

124
00:06:50,320 --> 00:06:52,760
all the way from very simple molecules

125
00:06:52,860 --> 00:06:56,560
that we would use in our gas grills for our barbecues

126
00:06:56,660 --> 00:06:59,800
to very heavy molecules that go into road tar.

127
00:06:59,801 --> 00:07:04,000
And gasoline is just a slice out of that carbon progression.

128
00:07:04,001 --> 00:07:08,380
But why do the six to ten carbon chains in gasoline work better in internal combustion

129
00:07:08,381 --> 00:07:13,240
engines than some heavier or lighter petroleum product?

130
00:07:13,241 --> 00:07:16,260
Because gasoline gives you the best of both worlds.

131
00:07:16,261 --> 00:07:20,860
It can be stored as a liquid, which takes up less room than a gas.

132
00:07:20,861 --> 00:07:26,660
But it vaporizes more easily than any other liquid hydrocarbon, a step the engine's combustion

133
00:07:26,661 --> 00:07:28,820
chamber can't do without.

134
00:07:28,821 --> 00:07:31,160
The liquid gasoline will not combust.

135
00:07:31,161 --> 00:07:33,160
It has to be in a vapor state.

136
00:07:33,161 --> 00:07:35,500
If you don't get proper vaporization at the time of

137
00:07:35,501 --> 00:07:39,460
combustion, you generally have what's termed a misfire.

138
00:07:39,461 --> 00:07:42,540
And of course, that results in poor performance, poor fuel

139
00:07:42,541 --> 00:07:46,100
economy, high emissions.

140
00:07:46,101 --> 00:07:48,300
So when you put the pedal to the metal,

141
00:07:48,301 --> 00:07:51,240
gasoline is pumped from the tank to the engine.

142
00:07:51,241 --> 00:07:54,840
It mixes with air just before entering the combustion chamber,

143
00:07:54,841 --> 00:07:57,280
changing the liquid to a vapor.

144
00:07:57,281 --> 00:08:01,520
The vapor is squeezed as the cylinder comes up for more power,

145
00:08:01,521 --> 00:08:05,180
and the spark plug ignites the mixture.

146
00:08:05,181 --> 00:08:07,840
The force of the explosion propels the piston down,

147
00:08:07,841 --> 00:08:12,120
turning the crankshaft, which is connected to the transmission,

148
00:08:12,121 --> 00:08:14,560
which uses gears to send the right amount of power

149
00:08:14,561 --> 00:08:16,340
to the wheels for smooth acceleration.

150
00:08:19,560 --> 00:08:21,880
But some gasolines provide better acceleration

151
00:08:21,881 --> 00:08:24,680
and overall performance than others,

152
00:08:24,681 --> 00:08:28,380
which is the reason for the range of prices at the pump.

153
00:08:28,381 --> 00:08:30,980
The biggest difference between the best grades of gasoline,

154
00:08:30,981 --> 00:08:35,120
usually called premium, ultimate, or supreme,

155
00:08:35,121 --> 00:08:39,780
and the middle and regular grades is the octane rating.

156
00:08:39,781 --> 00:08:41,480
It measures the ability of the fuel

157
00:08:41,481 --> 00:08:44,140
to be compressed without spontaneously igniting

158
00:08:44,141 --> 00:08:49,420
before the piston is in position and the spark plug fires.

159
00:08:49,421 --> 00:08:52,220
If you have octane that is too low in your gasoline,

160
00:08:52,221 --> 00:08:57,020
The gases are self-igniting before the piston can get to

161
00:08:57,021 --> 00:08:58,360
the top.

162
00:08:58,361 --> 00:09:01,920
And when this happens, this works against the power of your

163
00:09:01,921 --> 00:09:02,820
automobile.

164
00:09:02,821 --> 00:09:04,120
It decreases the power.

165
00:09:04,121 --> 00:09:07,480
Plus, it's very hard on the mechanical parts.

166
00:09:07,481 --> 00:09:09,980
Octane is the name of the eight-carbon molecule that is

167
00:09:09,981 --> 00:09:12,420
part of gasoline.

168
00:09:12,421 --> 00:09:15,560
It can handle compression without igniting so well that

169
00:09:15,561 --> 00:09:18,560
they named the rating scale after it.

170
00:09:18,561 --> 00:09:22,260
At the other end is 7-carbon heptane.

171
00:09:22,261 --> 00:09:25,720
Squeeze it just a little, and it'll ignite.

172
00:09:25,721 --> 00:09:29,820
Scientists in the late 20s created an artificial scale,

173
00:09:29,821 --> 00:09:34,720
assigning heptane a zero and octane a rating of 100.

174
00:09:34,721 --> 00:09:37,900
This way, unknown fuels could be tested and compared to pure

175
00:09:37,901 --> 00:09:41,600
octane or pure heptane.

176
00:09:41,601 --> 00:09:45,360
So a gasoline with an 87-octane rating

177
00:09:45,361 --> 00:09:51,360
it performs as if it contained 87% octane and 13% heptane,

178
00:09:51,361 --> 00:09:54,360
even though it actually contains molecules of many different

179
00:09:54,361 --> 00:09:57,360
lengths. The frustrating thing about octane

180
00:09:57,361 --> 00:10:02,360
is that low octane would please most engines most of the time.

181
00:10:02,361 --> 00:10:06,360
You only need that high octane a fraction of the time, during

182
00:10:06,361 --> 00:10:10,360
those extra bursts of power when accelerating.

183
00:10:10,361 --> 00:10:13,360
But long before anyone cared about octane, or even gasoline

184
00:10:13,361 --> 00:10:16,860
for that matter, there was another product that dominated

185
00:10:16,861 --> 00:10:19,860
the early oil business.

186
00:10:19,861 --> 00:10:23,860
Next, drilling the first oil well and the birth of the refining

187
00:10:23,861 --> 00:10:26,360
industry.

188
00:10:26,361 --> 00:10:30,360
In America, gasoline is the most widely used petroleum product.

189
00:10:30,361 --> 00:10:33,360
More than 40% of each barrel of oil is made into gasoline.

190
00:10:36,360 --> 00:10:39,860
Considering the wide range of products in addition to gasoline

191
00:10:39,861 --> 00:10:43,200
that can be derived from crude oil.

192
00:10:43,300 --> 00:10:45,060
It's no wonder people make such a big deal

193
00:10:45,160 --> 00:10:49,700
about the price of a barrel of this smelly, gooey substance.

194
00:10:49,800 --> 00:10:52,900
And the whole fuss started in 1859,

195
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:56,980
when a man named Edwin Drake drilled the first oil well.

196
00:10:57,080 --> 00:10:58,820
EDWIN DRAKE, The well was drilled in 1859

197
00:10:58,920 --> 00:11:01,620
to a total depth of 69 and 1⁄2 feet.

198
00:11:01,720 --> 00:11:05,260
Within less than a year, over 1,500 wells

199
00:11:05,360 --> 00:11:08,380
had been drilled in and around Titusville, Pennsylvania,

200
00:11:08,381 --> 00:11:13,380
primarily because oil was close and shallow near the surface.

201
00:11:13,480 --> 00:11:15,620
Experiments at the time had shown

202
00:11:15,720 --> 00:11:17,920
that boiling small amounts of crude oil

203
00:11:18,020 --> 00:11:20,520
that had already bubbled to the surface

204
00:11:20,620 --> 00:11:23,020
allowed chemists to collect a new product,

205
00:11:23,120 --> 00:11:26,440
known as kerosene, which could be used to light lamps.

206
00:11:29,440 --> 00:11:31,640
The drooling frenzy in Titusville began

207
00:11:31,740 --> 00:11:34,880
because Drake had proven there were vast amounts of oil

208
00:11:34,980 --> 00:11:38,380
farther underground.

209
00:11:38,381 --> 00:11:41,520
This great supply allowed kerosene to light lamps more

210
00:11:41,580 --> 00:11:43,920
cheaply than its blubbery competitor.

211
00:11:43,980 --> 00:11:46,520
Where whale blubber was becoming in short supply,

212
00:11:46,580 --> 00:11:50,320
the future of whale oil seemed in question.

213
00:11:50,400 --> 00:11:53,420
Suddenly, magically, somebody comes up with this product that

214
00:11:53,500 --> 00:11:57,260
can be made from something coming out from the ground.

215
00:11:57,340 --> 00:11:59,740
Kerosene was separated from the rest of the crude oil

216
00:11:59,800 --> 00:12:03,280
materials in primitive refineries.

217
00:12:03,340 --> 00:12:06,880
They were appropriately called stills.

218
00:12:06,881 --> 00:12:10,680
The early refineries were basically just like a drum

219
00:12:10,681 --> 00:12:12,620
with a tube sticking out of it.

220
00:12:12,960 --> 00:12:16,380
It was very similar to what the moonshiners used to do.

221
00:12:17,060 --> 00:12:18,960
The process, known as distillation,

222
00:12:20,240 --> 00:12:22,040
boiled oil until it vaporized.

223
00:12:23,220 --> 00:12:26,000
Then the kerosene vapors were collected and condensed

224
00:12:26,001 --> 00:12:27,240
back to liquid form.

225
00:12:29,620 --> 00:12:32,200
In the 1800s, people couldn't have cared less

226
00:12:32,201 --> 00:12:34,360
about the gasoline which boiled and condensed

227
00:12:34,361 --> 00:12:35,920
just before the kerosene did.

228
00:12:36,880 --> 00:12:39,520
Before there was an obvious market for gasoline,

229
00:12:39,620 --> 00:12:41,320
it was seen as a nuisance.

230
00:12:41,420 --> 00:12:44,620
And after the refining process had produced the most kerosene

231
00:12:44,720 --> 00:12:47,320
that you could get out of it, quite often the gasoline

232
00:12:47,420 --> 00:12:52,320
would be dumped into a nearby stream and float on away.

233
00:12:52,420 --> 00:12:54,640
Throughout the late 1800s, refining

234
00:12:54,740 --> 00:12:58,240
remained simple and was a cheap business to enter.

235
00:12:58,340 --> 00:13:00,640
Anyone could become a refiner.

236
00:13:00,740 --> 00:13:02,880
Then one refinery owner in Cleveland

237
00:13:02,980 --> 00:13:06,880
figured out how to outdo all of these small-time competitors.

238
00:13:06,881 --> 00:13:11,880
John D. Rockefeller said, if you can't beat them, then buy them.

239
00:13:11,881 --> 00:13:15,880
Instead of taking his profits early, he poured the money back

240
00:13:15,881 --> 00:13:19,880
into his company to buy refineries and the companies that

241
00:13:19,881 --> 00:13:21,880
transported oil and kerosene.

242
00:13:21,881 --> 00:13:25,880
The Rockefeller Trust, which was created in the late 1800s,

243
00:13:25,881 --> 00:13:31,880
was put in place primarily to take advantage of the large

244
00:13:31,881 --> 00:13:34,880
number of small independent producers.

245
00:13:34,881 --> 00:13:37,680
In other words, gathering these into a large entity.

246
00:13:37,780 --> 00:13:40,480
But because of the concentration of power,

247
00:13:40,580 --> 00:13:42,980
the concentration of all the resources,

248
00:13:43,080 --> 00:13:45,120
it really became a monopoly.

249
00:13:45,220 --> 00:13:48,420
By 1880, Rockefeller's company controlled 90%

250
00:13:48,520 --> 00:13:51,640
of America's refining capacity.

251
00:13:51,740 --> 00:13:54,840
But just as kerosene had bumped whale oil off the market

252
00:13:54,940 --> 00:13:59,640
years earlier, very soon Thomas Edison's new invention

253
00:13:59,740 --> 00:14:04,880
would turn out the lights on the kerosene business.

254
00:14:04,881 --> 00:14:08,880
Fortunately for Rockefeller, the various people tinkering in

255
00:14:08,881 --> 00:14:12,880
their garages with the first automobiles, found that gasoline

256
