00:29:22,681 --> 00:29:26,520
That equates to about 750 trucks that would be on the road.

509
00:29:26,521 --> 00:29:28,900
And when you think in terms of that many trucks on the road,

510
00:29:28,901 --> 00:29:31,020
there are issues with regard to safety.

511
00:29:31,021 --> 00:29:33,100
There are issues with regard to cost.

512
00:29:33,101 --> 00:29:36,900
The price of gasoline would be almost doubled, if not more.

513
00:29:36,901 --> 00:29:39,800
Pipeline is the most safe and efficient way

514
00:29:39,801 --> 00:29:42,880
of transporting barrels throughout the United States.

515
00:29:42,881 --> 00:29:46,300
By 1956, there were over 36,000 miles

516
00:29:46,301 --> 00:29:50,140
pipelines for gasoline in America.

517
00:29:50,141 --> 00:29:53,600
Today, there are more than 150,000 miles of these pipelines

518
00:29:53,601 --> 00:29:57,740
beneath our feet. Pump stations located every 50

519
00:29:57,741 --> 00:30:01,240
miles or so keep the fuel moving.

520
00:30:01,241 --> 00:30:04,460
Before computers, workers separated different products

521
00:30:04,461 --> 00:30:10,160
inside pipelines using small plungers known as pigs.

522
00:30:10,161 --> 00:30:12,760
Their name came from the fact that they would make a squealing

523
00:30:12,761 --> 00:30:15,600
sound as they moved through the pipes.

524
00:30:15,601 --> 00:30:18,800
And they're still used today for cleaning.

525
00:30:18,801 --> 00:30:20,000
This is a pipeline pig.

526
00:30:20,001 --> 00:30:21,800
This is a cleaning pig.

527
00:30:21,801 --> 00:30:25,000
This is a four inch in diameter, and these things come in various

528
00:30:25,001 --> 00:30:28,840
different sizes, as big as 60 inches in diameter.

529
00:30:28,841 --> 00:30:32,040
This actually travels along in the flow of the product.

530
00:30:32,041 --> 00:30:36,360
These cups are drive cups, so the fluid actually pushes the pig

531
00:30:36,361 --> 00:30:39,560
along the pipeline. Pipeline engineers have also

532
00:30:39,561 --> 00:30:43,600
developed smart pigs by attaching sophisticated electronic sensors

533
00:30:43,601 --> 00:30:47,600
long units. They don't squeal, but they do

534
00:30:47,601 --> 00:30:51,600
talk back to the controllers, telling them exactly where cracks

535
00:30:51,601 --> 00:30:57,600
may be developing so repairs can be made before a problem occurs.

536
00:30:57,601 --> 00:31:00,600
Gasoline and other products can now run continuously instead of

537
00:31:00,601 --> 00:31:05,600
shutting down to insert product separating pigs, thanks to the

538
00:31:05,601 --> 00:31:10,600
computerized flow controls at a handful of traffic control centers.

539
00:31:10,601 --> 00:31:14,040
Here, they keep the amount of mixing between products

540
00:31:14,140 --> 00:31:15,240
to a minimum.

541
00:31:15,340 --> 00:31:17,040
We have what's called a batching system.

542
00:31:17,140 --> 00:31:18,540
We'll batch gasoline.

543
00:31:18,640 --> 00:31:22,940
We'll batch premium and regular, diesel fuel, jet fuel, et cetera.

544
00:31:23,040 --> 00:31:25,240
And we don't put anything in between those.

545
00:31:25,340 --> 00:31:27,660
There is some co-mingling of the product,

546
00:31:27,760 --> 00:31:30,260
and that's put into what's called a transmix tank.

547
00:31:30,360 --> 00:31:32,660
And then that's sold to a different market.

548
00:31:32,760 --> 00:31:35,260
Some of that mixture is sent back to refineries

549
00:31:35,360 --> 00:31:38,260
to be separated once again.

550
00:31:38,261 --> 00:31:41,700
The rest is sold to industries that can use any type of fuel

551
00:31:41,701 --> 00:31:44,900
to power their equipment. Pipelines are operated in one

552
00:31:44,901 --> 00:31:49,500
of two ways. 95% work as common carriers,

553
00:31:49,501 --> 00:31:54,700
and 5% are known as dedicated. For instance, a pipeline running

554
00:31:54,701 --> 00:31:58,380
from a Chevron refinery to a Chevron terminal, where Chevron

555
00:31:58,381 --> 00:32:02,620
trucks deliver gasoline to Chevron stations, is a dedicated

556
00:32:02,621 --> 00:32:06,620
system. But the other 95% of pipelines,

557
00:32:06,621 --> 00:32:09,620
especially long ones running all the way from Houston to the

558
00:32:09,621 --> 00:32:14,620
upper Midwest or East Coast are common carrier pipelines.

559
00:32:14,621 --> 00:32:18,620
Several companies buy time from the pipeline operator.

560
00:32:18,621 --> 00:32:22,620
This system leads to a very interesting fact that gasoline

561
00:32:22,621 --> 00:32:26,620
companies don't exactly volunteer to consumers.

562
00:32:26,621 --> 00:32:31,620
All gasoline is basically the same, or as the industry says,

563
00:32:31,621 --> 00:32:36,860
as the industry says, fungible, a fancy word for generic.

564
00:32:36,861 --> 00:32:40,800
When Shell puts in a barrel of gasoline in Houston, Texas,

565
00:32:40,801 --> 00:32:44,440
that barrel that Shell put in is not necessarily

566
00:32:44,441 --> 00:32:48,000
the same wet barrel that they'll get out in Chicago

567
00:32:48,001 --> 00:32:50,100
or some other location.

568
00:32:50,101 --> 00:32:52,140
But it is of the same specification

569
00:32:52,141 --> 00:32:53,840
required by all shippers.

570
00:32:53,841 --> 00:32:59,740
A shipper can put a barrel into our system at Baytown, Texas,

571
00:32:59,741 --> 00:33:04,140
refinery today and take a barrel out of the system in the Midwest

572
00:33:04,141 --> 00:33:06,140
or Northeast on the same day.

573
00:33:06,141 --> 00:33:09,380
So they don't have to wait the 13-day transit time

574
00:33:09,381 --> 00:33:11,280
on our system.

575
00:33:11,281 --> 00:33:13,380
In this shared pipeline system,

576
00:33:13,381 --> 00:33:16,800
one company's regular unleaded or super unleaded

577
00:33:16,801 --> 00:33:20,200
is the same as another's.

578
00:33:20,201 --> 00:33:22,900
The last chance companies have to put their personal stamp

579
00:33:22,901 --> 00:33:28,700
on gasoline is at the terminal by mixing in chemical additives.

580
00:33:28,701 --> 00:33:32,000
There are the qualities that you want to make your particular

581
00:33:32,001 --> 00:33:33,700
product stand out.

582
00:33:33,701 --> 00:33:35,500
That's done by additives.

583
00:33:35,501 --> 00:33:38,500
And so there's an enormous amount of research going into

584
00:33:38,501 --> 00:33:41,740
additives that will clean the engine, that will stop

585
00:33:41,741 --> 00:33:45,260
corrosion, that will reduce the amount of wear.

586
00:33:45,261 --> 00:33:48,060
The only problem is that all of the chemical additives do

587
00:33:48,061 --> 00:33:51,760
basically those same few things, as well as boosting

588
00:33:51,761 --> 00:33:54,060
octane and lowering emissions.

589
00:33:54,061 --> 00:33:56,360
That means it's up to the marketing department to convince

590
00:33:56,361 --> 00:34:02,100
consumers that you can put a tiger in your tank or drive your

591
00:34:02,101 --> 00:34:05,260
engine clean if you use the right brand.

592
00:34:05,261 --> 00:34:08,400
For a time historically, there were dramatic differences

593
00:34:08,401 --> 00:34:11,000
produced by different refining processes.

594
00:34:11,001 --> 00:34:15,420
But as time passes by the 60s, 70s, and on into the present,

595
00:34:15,421 --> 00:34:19,220
those differences get a lot less so that most companies, while

596
00:34:19,221 --> 00:34:21,920
they would like to advertise how much better their gasoline is

597
00:34:21,921 --> 00:34:24,520
than their competitors, are selling essentially the same

598
00:34:24,521 --> 00:34:25,920
product.

599
00:34:25,921 --> 00:34:29,220
The additives are stored separately at terminals.

600
00:34:29,221 --> 00:34:32,860
Pipelines from refineries feed directly into temporary gasoline

601
00:34:32,861 --> 00:34:37,160
tanks, capable of holding 50,000 gallons each.

602
00:34:37,161 --> 00:34:41,220
When a truck pulls up to what is known as the rack to be filled,

603
00:34:41,221 --> 00:34:44,420
pumps move the proper grade of gasoline out of the right storage

604
00:34:44,421 --> 00:34:48,220
tank and into the truck. The chemical additives kept in

605
00:34:48,221 --> 00:34:52,820
smaller tanks are put in at the last second, so mixtures can be

606
00:34:52,821 --> 00:34:56,220
be precisely controlled. Our trucks have a capacity of about

607
00:34:56,221 --> 00:34:59,660
9,000 gallons. With the system that we have here with the pumps

608
00:34:59,661 --> 00:35:05,320
and our loading rack layout, we can load our truck in approximately 10 to 15 minutes.

609
00:35:05,321 --> 00:35:09,820
And that's from the time our driver pulls onto the rack and pulls off of the rack.

610
00:35:09,821 --> 00:35:15,780
From there, the drivers are off to the service station.

611
00:35:15,781 --> 00:35:20,940
Each truck is separated into three or four compartments, so different grades of gasoline

612
00:35:20,941 --> 00:35:23,280
can be delivered to underground storage tanks.

613
00:35:27,280 --> 00:35:29,380
This incredibly efficient delivery system

614
00:35:29,381 --> 00:35:31,440
helps keep gasoline prices low.

615
00:35:34,340 --> 00:35:37,580
But if gasoline is to remain the fuel of choice,

616
00:35:37,581 --> 00:35:40,940
it's going to have to compete with emerging lower emission

617
00:35:40,941 --> 00:35:42,600
alternatives.

618
00:35:42,601 --> 00:35:48,780
Next, the future, gasoline versus the alternatives.

619
00:35:48,781 --> 00:35:53,160
Large gasoline terminals can load 2 million gallons a day

620
00:35:53,161 --> 00:35:55,720
and operate 365 days a year.

621
00:36:00,320 --> 00:36:03,820
Today, gasoline is designed to produce less pollution

622
00:36:03,821 --> 00:36:05,360
than ever before.

623
00:36:05,361 --> 00:36:09,300
Special formulas of gasoline with reshaped molecules

624
00:36:09,301 --> 00:36:13,140
and catalytic converters on automobile exhaust systems

625
00:36:13,141 --> 00:36:16,540
both help cars run cleaner.

626
00:36:16,541 --> 00:36:19,680
But each gallon of gas still releases about five pounds of

627
00:36:19,681 --> 00:36:24,440
carbon and other pollutants into the atmosphere, mostly as

628
00:36:24,441 --> 00:36:28,680
invisible carbon dioxide gas. There's no question that

629
00:36:28,681 --> 00:36:34,500
lower emissions is the focus of fuel development today.

630
00:36:34,501 --> 00:36:39,300
We've made great strides over the past 30 or 40 years, but the

631
00:36:39,301 --> 00:36:42,440
greatest challenge for gasoline in the future is to maintain its

632
00:36:42,441 --> 00:36:46,300
focus on reduced emissions. Researchers are

633
00:36:46,301 --> 00:36:49,300
testing different formulas of gasoline in engines under a

634
00:36:49,301 --> 00:36:53,300
variety of conditions to find the best combination of

635
00:36:53,301 --> 00:36:58,300
performance, reliability, and low emissions.

636
00:36:58,301 --> 00:37:02,300
At the conclusion of a test, we will tear the engines down,

637
00:37:02,301 --> 00:37:06,300
take a look inside, weigh specific parts, for example,

638
00:37:06,301 --> 00:37:09,300
to measure different performance features, and then evaluate

639
00:37:09,301 --> 00:37:14,300
our product and how to make improvements on that product.

640
00:37:14,301 --> 00:37:18,300
Besides being made cleaner, gasoline can also be mixed with

641
00:37:18,301 --> 00:37:21,540
other materials to reduce pollution.

642
00:37:21,541 --> 00:37:26,300
Combining gasoline with ethanol, and alcohol made from corn or

643
00:37:26,301 --> 00:37:29,300
other crops, is one way of slightly lowering emissions

644
00:37:29,301 --> 00:37:32,300
while also decreasing the overall consumption of

645
00:37:32,301 --> 00:37:35,300
gasoline. Cars in the Midwest already run

646
00:37:35,301 --> 00:37:40,300
on mixtures containing about 10% to 15% ethanol.

647
00:37:40,301 --> 00:37:43,300
But gasoline isn't the only current vehicle fuel that can

648
00:37:43,301 --> 00:37:45,700
fuel that can be mixed with other products to lower

649
00:37:45,701 --> 00:37:49,200
emissions and reduce oil use.

650
00:37:49,201 --> 00:37:51,500
Diesel fuel can be combined with something known as

651
00:37:51,501 --> 00:37:55,740
biodiesel to achieve similar results.

652
00:37:55,741 --> 00:38:00,360
Biodiesel is produced from living plants,

653
00:38:00,361 --> 00:38:04,360
such as soy bean oil, and also can be produced from

654
00:38:04,361 --> 00:38:05,360
animal fat.

655
00:38:05,361 --> 00:38:07,460
Think of the grocery store, a bottle of general

656
00:38:07,461 --> 00:38:09,500
purpose vegetable oil.

657
00:38:09,501 --> 00:38:12,500
It isn't the raw oil from crushing the seeds.

658
00:38:12,501 --> 00:38:15,900
The material has been processed and refined

659
00:38:16,000 --> 00:38:19,100
to make it into a product that we would use for cooking.

660
00:38:19,200 --> 00:38:21,740
And the same type improvement of the oil through refining

661
00:38:21,840 --> 00:38:25,680
makes it into a material that's suitable as a diesel fuel.

662
00:38:25,780 --> 00:38:27,540
Since gasoline and diesel can both

663
00:38:27,640 --> 00:38:29,520
be mixed with lower emission materials

664
00:38:29,620 --> 00:38:32,460
made from renewable sources, it's

665
00:38:32,560 --> 00:38:36,360
hard to determine which combination makes more sense.

666
00:38:36,460 --> 00:38:38,400
The basic design of a diesel engine

667
00:38:38,500 --> 00:38:41,600
may eventually give that fuel the edge.

668
00:38:41,601 --> 00:38:45,360
The biggest difference is that diesel doesn't require a spark plug.

669
00:38:45,361 --> 00:38:48,720
We squeeze the diesel fuel down to a smaller volume.

670
00:38:48,721 --> 00:38:55,200
That generates more heat, and that heat is then able to ignite the diesel without a spark.

671
00:38:55,201 --> 00:39:02,160
Because of the higher compression ratio, the diesel engine will be on average about 30% more efficient.

672
00:39:02,161 --> 00:39:07,680
Biodiesel and ethanol can also power their respective engines entirely on their own,

673
00:39:07,681 --> 00:39:10,720
with only minor modifications.

674
00:39:10,721 --> 00:39:14,160
This would lower emissions even further and drastically reduce

675
00:39:14,161 --> 00:39:19,160
oil dependency. But these biofuels may be hard

676
00:39:19,161 --> 00:39:22,960
pressed to meet the demand for a huge number of cars.

677
00:39:22,961 --> 00:39:27,200
And most studies that I have seen suggest that the amount of

678
00:39:27,201 --> 00:39:31,440
crop land they require just exceeds the available crop land

679
00:39:31,441 --> 00:39:37,920
we have, even in a land abundant country such as the United States.

680
00:39:37,921 --> 00:39:41,060
Since alternative gasoline and diesel mixtures still release

681
00:39:41,061 --> 00:39:46,960
pollution, the search continues for a true zero emission answer.

682
00:39:46,961 --> 00:39:50,560
Electric cars don't pollute, but drivers can usually only drive

683
00:39:50,561 --> 00:39:54,760
about 100 miles before they have to stop to recharge.

684
00:39:54,761 --> 00:40:00,420
So gasoline still helps out in the form of hybrid vehicles.

685
00:40:00,421 --> 00:40:05,160
These cars have two engines, internal combustion and electric.

686
00:40:05,161 --> 00:40:08,000
Hybrids are gaining wider acceptance as costs from a two

687
00:40:08,001 --> 00:40:11,560
engine system begin to come down.

688
00:40:11,561 --> 00:40:15,260
Since less power is needed from the internal combustion engine,

689
00:40:15,261 --> 00:40:20,200
smaller, less wasteful models can be used.

690
00:40:20,201 --> 00:40:23,600
The electric motor does a lot of the work, and in most systems

691
00:40:23,601 --> 00:40:26,720
is recharged during normal driving by the power generated

692
00:40:26,721 --> 00:40:30,760
when the car breaks, or by the internal combustion engine

693
00:40:30,761 --> 00:40:32,120
if needed.

694
00:40:32,121 --> 00:40:34,920
Therefore, less gasoline is used.

695
00:40:34,921 --> 00:40:38,220
This means less pollutants are released into the atmosphere,

696
00:40:38,221 --> 00:40:40,560
compared to cars powered solely by gasoline.

697
00:40:43,420 --> 00:40:46,300
Besides electric motors, fuel cells

698
00:40:46,301 --> 00:40:48,560
are another possible zero emissions alternative

699
00:40:48,561 --> 00:40:51,900
to gasoline-powered engines.

700
00:40:51,901 --> 00:40:54,280
In order to eliminate any pollution,

701
00:40:54,281 --> 00:40:57,680
pure hydrogen must be fed into the fuel cell.

702
00:40:57,681 --> 00:41:02,320
A special membrane separates hydrogen electrons and protons.

703
00:41:02,321 --> 00:41:05,360
The electrons are forced to travel around the membrane.

704
00:41:05,460 --> 00:41:08,120
This movement creates electricity.

705
00:41:08,220 --> 00:41:10,460
Then they rejoin with the protons,

706
00:41:10,560 --> 00:41:13,560
and the hydrogen combines with available oxygen.

707
00:41:13,660 --> 00:41:16,260
In this case, the only exhaust is water.

708
00:41:18,460 --> 00:41:20,980
Truly zero emissions.

709
00:41:21,080 --> 00:41:24,740
The problem is that pure hydrogen isn't so easy to come by.

710
00:41:24,840 --> 00:41:27,920
Hydrogen is cited by many scientists

711
00:41:28,020 --> 00:41:31,220
as our most abundant element on Earth, which it is.

712
00:41:31,221 --> 00:41:34,420
But the hydrogen that exists on Earth is combined with other

713
00:41:34,421 --> 00:41:38,660
elements, primarily as a hydrocarbon or as water.

714
00:41:38,661 --> 00:41:41,700
Pure hydrogen can be separated from other materials at special

715
00:41:41,701 --> 00:41:45,300
plants and is already being shipped to a small number of

716
00:41:45,301 --> 00:41:49,380
experimental hydrogen refilling stations.

717
00:41:49,381 --> 00:41:52,340
But it will take some time before pure hydrogen is available

718
00:41:52,341 --> 00:41:55,140
everywhere that gasoline is now.

719
00:41:55,141 --> 00:41:57,940
When we drive around, we don't think about the network that

720
00:41:57,941 --> 00:42:02,780
gasoline to our cities. When people talk about moving to

721
00:42:02,781 --> 00:42:06,280
the hydrogen economy, for example, that would require us

722
00:42:06,281 --> 00:42:09,780
to start all over again from scratch.

723
00:42:09,781 --> 00:42:13,280
So just as in hybrid electric cars, gasoline is

724
00:42:13,281 --> 00:42:17,100
sneaking into the fuel cell scenario since it contains a lot

725
00:42:17,101 --> 00:42:20,600
of hydrogen. Powering fuel cells with the

726
00:42:20,601 --> 00:42:25,100
hydrogen found in gasoline would still produce some emissions.

727
00:42:25,101 --> 00:42:27,940
But since fuel cells are much more efficient than internal

728
00:42:27,941 --> 00:42:31,440
combustion engines, you'll be able to go a lot farther on

729
00:42:31,441 --> 00:42:34,440
that gallon of gas.

730
00:42:34,441 --> 00:42:37,440
An alternative that wouldn't require any infrastructure

731
00:42:37,441 --> 00:42:39,340
is solar power.

732
00:42:39,341 --> 00:42:42,060
It can already propel light research vehicles and may be

733
00:42:42,061 --> 00:42:46,760
used in the near future to provide part of a car's power.

734
00:42:46,761 --> 00:42:49,800
But it will be some time before researchers can squeeze enough

735
00:42:49,801 --> 00:42:55,100
horsepower from solar cells to fuel a heavy consumer car.

736
00:42:55,101 --> 00:42:58,800
are, since the more immediately viable electric and fuel cell

737
00:42:58,801 --> 00:43:02,700
options still involve the use of gasoline.

738
00:43:02,701 --> 00:43:07,140
It seems this familiar fuel will be with us for some time.

739
00:43:07,141 --> 00:43:11,860
Gasoline has come a long way in 100 years, and apparently still

740
00:43:11,861 --> 00:43:15,160
has a long way to go. We've thoroughly

741
00:43:15,161 --> 00:43:17,560
adopted gasoline as a fundamental part of our

742
00:43:17,561 --> 00:43:20,800
economy, the fundamental driving force in our

743
00:43:20,801 --> 00:43:23,800
transportation system. What happens in the future will

744
00:43:23,801 --> 00:43:27,700
will depend greatly on continuous technological advances

745
00:43:27,800 --> 00:43:30,540
and competing processes.

746
00:43:30,640 --> 00:43:33,040
The fuel race is on.

747
00:43:33,140 --> 00:43:35,740
Gasoline will keep getting cleaner.

748
00:43:35,840 --> 00:43:38,340
However, it seems likely that other fuels

749
00:43:38,440 --> 00:43:41,060
will eventually overcome their technical limitations

750
00:43:41,160 --> 00:43:45,160
or supply problems to surpass it.

751
00:43:45,260 --> 00:43:50,060
But in today's world, gasoline still reigns supreme.

